<h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A particle of mass m is moving in a circular path of radius r under the action of a centripetal force F = - <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">k</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> where k is a positive constant. The total energy of the particle is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) -<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">k</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">k</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) -<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">k</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">k</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">r</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> A block of mass m is placed on a rough inclined plane of inclination &theta; keeping horizontal. If the coefficient of static friction is &mu;<sub>s</sub>, the minimum force F required to move the block up the inclined plane is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) mg sin&theta;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) mg(sin&theta; + &mu;<sub>s</sub> cos&theta;)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) mg(sin&theta; - &mu;<sub>s</sub> cos&theta;)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg cos&theta;</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Three blocks of masses m<sub>1</sub>, m<sub>2</sub> and m<sub>3</sub> are connected as shown in the system. If a force F is applied to mass m<sub>3</sub>, the tension T<sub>1</sub> between m<sub>1</sub> and m<sub>2</sub> is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> F</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>3</sub></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">(m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)F</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>3</sub></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>3</sub> F</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>3</sub></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">(m<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>3</sub>)F</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>3</sub></span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A bullet of mass 0.05 kg moving with a speed of 200 m/s enters a heavy wooden block and is stopped after a distance of 0.5 m. What is the average resistive force exerted by the block on the bullet? <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2000 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 4000 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 200 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 400 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> A rocket with a lift-off mass 20,000 kg is blasted upwards with an initial acceleration of 5.0 m/s<sup>2</sup>. The initial thrust of the blast is (g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>): <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 2.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 3.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 3.5 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> A body of mass 2 kg travels according to the relation x(t) = pt<sup>3</sup> + qt<sup>2</sup> + rt, where constants are p = 3 m/s<sup>3</sup>, q = 4 m/s<sup>2</sup>, and r = 5 m/s. The force acting on the body at t = 2 s is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 64 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 32 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 128 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 16 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> The coefficient of friction between a tire and a road is 0.4. The maximum safe speed of a car of mass 1000 kg to negotiate a circular curve of radius 100 m without skidding is (g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>): <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 20 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 40 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 10 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 15 m/s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A chain of mass M and length L is hanging over a smooth table with 1/nth of its length hanging freely. The work done in pulling the hanging part back on the table is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">MgL</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">n<sup>2</sup></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">MgL</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2n<sup>2</sup></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">MgL</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">n</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">MgL</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2n</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> An elevator accelerates upwards with an acceleration a. A person inside the elevator weighs a body using a spring balance. The reading of the spring balance compared to the true weight is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) less than true weight</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) greater than true weight</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) equal to true weight</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>id) zero</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q10.</strong> A force vector applied on a mass is represented as <b>F&#8407;</b> = (6i&#x0302; - 8j&#x0302; + 10k&#x0302;) N and accelerates the mass at 1 m/s<sup>2</sup>. What is the mass of the body? <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 10&radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">2</span> kg</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 2&radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">10</span> kg</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 10 kg</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 100 kg</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate words or expressions.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The property of a body by virtue of which it opposes any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called _______. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The impulse of a force is equal to the change in _______ of the body. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The maximum value of static friction is called _______ friction. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> For a banking angle &theta; on a curved road of radius r, the optimum speed to avoid wear and tear of tires is given by v = _______. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section C: Assertion-Reason Type Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Read the assertion and reason carefully and choose the correct option: (a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion. (b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion. (c) Assertion is true but Reason is false. (d) Assertion is false and Reason is true.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Assertion (A): A rocket works on the principle of conservation of linear momentum.
Reason (B): The total momentum of an isolated system of interacting bodies remains conserved. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false and Reason is true.</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Assertion (A): It is easier to pull a heavy lawn roller than to push it.
Reason (B): Pulling creates a component of force acting upwards, which decreases the effective normal reaction and hence the limiting friction. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false and Reason is true.</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section D: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer all questions in brief.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State Newton's second law of motion in terms of momentum and write its mathematical form in vector notation. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Why are wheels provided with mudguards? Explain briefly on the basis of laws of motion. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Define angle of friction. Prove that the coefficient of static friction is equal to the tangent of the angle of friction. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A batsman deflects a ball by an angle of 60<sup>\circ</sup> without changing its initial speed of 54 km/h. Calculate the impulse imparted to the ball if its mass is 0.15 kg. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section E: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer all questions with proper derivations or step-by-step solutions.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State and prove the Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum using Newton's third law of motion. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Derive an expression for the maximum safe speed of a vehicle on a banked circular road of radius r and banking angle &theta;, taking friction into account. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Two masses m<sub>1</sub> = 4 kg and m<sub>2</sub> = 6 kg are connected by a light string passing over a frictionless fixed pulley. Find the acceleration of the masses and the tension in the string when the masses are released. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Explain why centripetal force is necessary for circular motion. A stone of mass 1 kg tied to the end of a string of length 1 m is whirled in a horizontal circle at a angular speed of 2 rad/s. What is the maximum tension the string can bear if it breaks at a breaking tension of 10 N? Compute the maximum angular velocity. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section F: Long Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Provide detailed answers with complete derivations and numerical steps.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> (a) Define friction. Distinguish between static friction, kinetic friction, and rolling friction.
(b) A block of mass 10 kg rests on a horizontal plane. The coefficient of static friction is 0.5 and kinetic friction is 0.3. Calculate the frictional force acting on the block if a horizontal force of 20 N is applied, and also when the applied force becomes 60 N. (g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>) <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[5M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> (a) Using free-body diagrams, find the acceleration of a body sliding down a rough inclined plane making an angle &theta; with the horizontal, where &mu;<sub>k</sub> is the coefficient of kinetic friction.
(b) A block takes twice as much time to slide down a 45<sup>\circ</sup> rough incline as it takes to slide down a frictionless 45<sup>\circ</sup> incline of the same length. Calculate the coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the rough incline. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[5M]</span></div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section G: Case Study Based Question</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Read the passage carefully and answer the questions that follow.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Read the following passage on Newton's laws of motion and dynamic equilibrium, and answer the sub-questions below:

Newton's first law defines inertial frames of reference and states that a body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion unless acted upon by an external net force. Newton's second law quantifies force as the rate of change of linear momentum. In many real-world engineering problems, systems are analyzed using connected bodies, pulleys, lifts, and inclined planes where constraints dictate the motion. When all forces balance out, the body is in translational equilibrium. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[4M]</span></div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>What is an inertial frame of reference? Give one example.</li><li>Why is Newton's first law considered not merely a special case of Newton's second law, but fundamental?</li><li>A lift of mass 500 kg is ascending with an upward acceleration of 2 m/s<sup>2</sup>. Calculate the tension in the supporting cable. (g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>)</li></ol>
<div style='margin-top:30px;text-align:center;font-size:9pt;font-style:italic;color:#666;border-top:1px solid #ccc;padding-top:8px;'>
